---
/**
 * Composition — how this guide is built, shown with the guide's own components.
 *
 * The fourth page, and the only self-referential one. Every example on it is the
 * real component imported and rendered, never a picture of one: a page that
 * argued for composition using screenshots of composition would be the joke
 * about itself.
 *
 * Two movements. First the parts, small and all together, so somebody about to
 * ask for a new one can see what already exists. Then the situations the project
 * actually had to answer for, at full size, because those are the part a list of
 * components cannot tell you.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro'
import PartsGallery from '../components/PartsGallery.astro'
import Specimen from '../components/Specimen.astro'
import Section from '../components/Section.astro'
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro'
import DefinitionRow from '../components/DefinitionRow.astro'
import LabelledList from '../components/LabelledList.astro'
import RoleBadge from '../components/RoleBadge.astro'
import RichText from '../components/RichText.astro'
import Text from '../components/ui/Text.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro'
import { componentCount, composition, pageFrame, statesNotDrawn } from '../fixtures/inventory'
import { skills } from '../fixtures/skills'
import { auditRules } from '../fixtures/audit-rules'

/** The rows the examples are built from, taken from the fixtures so they cannot drift. */
const sharedSkill = skills.find((skill) => skill.readBy === 'both') ?? skills[0]
const oneSkill = skills.find((skill) => skill.readBy !== 'both') ?? skills[0]
const oneRule = auditRules.find((rule) => rule.id === 'arbitrary-scale') ?? auditRules[0]

const AUDIT_OUTPUT = `  arbitrary-scale - 135 in 43 places
   27 x  text-[13px]        components/AppSidebarItem.astro:34 +26
   14 x  rounded-[8px]      components/AppSidebarItem.astro:34 +13`

const WAIVER = `<!-- ui-audit-allow: inline-style — third-party embed, the vendor sets the style -->`

const PRINT_CHANGES = [
  'The background becomes true white: warm stone on paper is a dirty smudge.',
  'Body text drops to 11pt, which on paper is still generous.',
  'Code blocks wrap, because paper does not scroll.',
  'The small things stay whole — a case, a code block, a row — and no heading is left alone at the foot of a page.',
]

const PRINT_KEPT = [
  'The two accents: they are how you tell whose stretch of the page a part is.',
  'The vertical rail, and the marks hung on it.',
  'Full contrast. No grey on grey, which printed disappears.',
]
---

<Page
  title="Composition — cairn"
  description={`How this guide is built: the ${componentCount} components it is made of, and the situations it had to answer for.`}
>
  <PageIntro
    title="Composition"
    lead={`This guide is made of ${componentCount} parts. They are below — the real ones, running.`}
  >
    <Text class="mt-6 max-w-2xl">
      If you are about to inherit these components, or to ask for a new one, this page tells you
      what already exists. The examples are not pictures: they are the real components, imported.
      When one changes, this changes with it — and it cannot go quietly out of date.
    </Text>
  </PageIntro>

  <div class="mt-16 space-y-16">
    <Section title="The parts">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        The primitives are these: the text, the code, the mark, the block, the card, the number.
        Everything else is built from them, and none of them exists as two slightly different
        copies — which is the thing the check will not let past without a signature.
      </Text>

      <div class="mt-8 border-t border-rule">
        {composition.map((count) => <Figure value={count.value} label={count.label} />)}
      </div>

      <PartsGallery class="mt-8" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="The parts that do not fit in a case">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        That leaves {pageFrame.length} of the {componentCount}, and they cannot be put in a box
        halfway down the page: they are the page you are reading. Drawing them again inside a case
        would mean building them twice, which is exactly what this guide exists to avoid. So it
        points at them instead.
      </Text>

      <dl class="mt-8 border-t border-rule">
        {pageFrame.map((part) => <DefinitionRow term={part.name}>{part.where}</DefinitionRow>)}
      </dl>
    </Section>

    <Section title="One thing that belongs to both" tone="designer">
      <RoleBadge slot="eyebrow" role="both" />

      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        Two of the five skills are read by both roles. The short road is to give them a third
        colour. But this is not a third case of the same thing: it is the two at once, and an
        invented colour would hide that rather than say it. So the mark becomes two marks, and the
        row carries more weight.
      </Text>

      <Specimen
        name="RoleBadge · DefinitionRow"
        layout="full"
        class="mt-8"
        note="The `ui-composition` row, the same one on the Reference page."
      >
        <dl class="border-t border-rule">
          <DefinitionRow term={sharedSkill.name} emphasis="strong">
            <RoleBadge slot="aside" role={sharedSkill.readBy} />
            {sharedSkill.when}
          </DefinitionRow>
        </dl>
      </Specimen>

      <Text class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
        Two of the five rows are like this. A page tested only on designer and on developer would
        have gone to print without noticing.
      </Text>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two different lists, the same row">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        The five skills and the ten audit rules have nothing in common but their shape: a term, and
        what it means. So they are not two parts, they are one part holding different data.
      </Text>

      <Specimen
        name="DefinitionRow"
        layout="full"
        class="mt-8"
        note="A skill above, an audit rule below. Same component, two sources."
      >
        <dl class="border-t border-rule">
          <DefinitionRow term={oneSkill.name}>
            <RoleBadge slot="aside" role={oneSkill.readBy} />
            {oneSkill.when}
          </DefinitionRow>
          <DefinitionRow term={oneRule.id}><RichText copy={oneRule.catches} /></DefinitionRow>
        </dl>
      </Specimen>

      <Text class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
        Written twice they would diverge the first time somebody adjusted the spacing on one of
        them. It takes weeks, and nobody notices when it happened.
      </Text>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Wider than the line" tone="developer">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        A tool's output is made of columns, and wrapping it breaks them: so it scrolls. A long line
        of code is only long, and it wraps the way any editor would. Two behaviours of one part,
        not two parts.
      </Text>

      <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
        <CodeBlock label="tool output: it scrolls" code={AUDIT_OUTPUT} />
        <CodeBlock label="a single line: it wraps" code={WAIVER} wrap="soft" />
      </div>

      <Text variant="note" class="mt-6 max-w-2xl">
        Printed, both wrap, because paper does not scroll. That rule lives in the token file, not
        here.
      </Text>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Empty, loading, error">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        Every screen has to answer for its four states. Here the answer to three of them is that
        they do not exist — and the difference between that and forgetting them is that the reason
        is written down.
      </Text>

      <dl class="mt-8 border-t border-rule">
        {statesNotDrawn.map((state) => <DefinitionRow term={state.name}>{state.why}</DefinitionRow>)}
      </dl>

      <Text class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
        The day these lists stop being typed by hand and get read from the project's own folders,
        all three states become real, and none of them is drawn. That day is already noted in the
        handoff, with the number of the open question.
      </Text>
    </Section>

    <Section title="On paper">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        This guide gets printed, and projected in meetings: two surfaces worse than a laptop
        screen. So print is a designed format here, not whatever comes out by accident.
      </Text>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <LabelledList title="what changes in print" items={PRINT_CHANGES} />
        <LabelledList title="what stays as it is" items={PRINT_KEPT} />
      </div>

      <Text variant="note" class="mt-6 max-w-2xl">
        Try it now: Cmd+P, look at the preview, and close it without printing anything.
      </Text>
    </Section>
  </div>
</Page>
